        <div class="bg-[#111] border border-white/5 rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Our Verdict</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">Cloudzy (formerly RouterHosting) occupies a useful and underappreciated position in the 2026 VPS market: an 18-year-old, self-funded, independent operator that has not raised a single dollar of VC money and has not been acquired, yet still ships modern infrastructure (DDR5 + EPYC + NVMe + 40 Gbps) at competitive prices. The Dubai/UAE Free Zone company registration is a real differentiator for buyers who specifically want non-US, non-EU jurisdiction, and the public Monero (XMR) acceptance is a privacy configuration most legacy providers do not offer. The pitch will resonate strongly with developers, game-server admins, and trading-bot operators who care about single-thread CPU performance (the Ryzen 4.2 GHz+ line) and modern memory/networking more than absolute cheapest $/GB.</p>
          <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mt-4">The biggest caveat is the 50% promotional pricing in effect at the time of writing. The standard rates shown alongside each tier are roughly 2x promotional (e.g. $4.95 standard vs $2.48 promo for the entry tier), and renewals revert to standard unless explicitly negotiated. Buyers who don't read the fine print may experience renewal sticker-shock. For buyers who want a stable, predictable, promotional-locked price, <a href="/vps/racknerd" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">RackNerd</a>'s $10.98/yr annual special is a more honest value comparison. For buyers who want modern infrastructure + multi-DC + 18-year self-funded track record + crypto billing, Cloudzy is the cleanest match in this comparison set.</p>
        </div>

